<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khyber Pass</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khyber Pass is a mountain pass linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> with the plains of the north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. It has long been a key route for armies and trade entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> used it during his invasion of India, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> extended its control towards the pass in the nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khyber-pass.jpg" alt="Khyber Pass" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Mollison · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KhyberPassPakistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
